## Introduction
Organic–inorganic hybrid perovskite solar cells (PSCs) have garnered considerable attention as promising next-generation photovoltaic devices owing to their remarkable properties, including broad bandgap tunability and low-cost solution processing.[1-4] The certified power conversion efficiency (PCE) of PSCs has reached $25.8\%$ for single-junction cells operating at $300~\mathsf{K}$ .[5] Recently, it has been proven that PSCs can work at low temperatures (220–290 K), which fulfills the requirements for space applications such as light weight, high performance with high specific power, and compatibility with flexible fabrication.[6-7] Therefore, PSCs may open up promising applications for near-space and polarregion use, and it is of particular interest to achieve exceptionally high-quality perovskite films to transit this technology for use in real applications. However, the ionic nature of perovskites inevitably generates abundant defects during crystallization, which act as nonradiative recombination centers to limit photovoltaic performance.[8] Although previous studies found that the phase transition and lattice distortion in perovskite films during temperature cycling can partially eliminate intrinsic defects,[9-10] the efficiency is still much lower, at $25.2\%$ (stabilized at $24.2\%$ at $220~\mathsf{K},$ because of the large amount of intrinsic defects generated in perovskite films that are mainly induced by stacking faults during crystallization.
Earlier reports on methylammonium lead iodide (MAPbI3) have demonstrated how conversion of precursors to crystals, including phase conversion pathways and nucleation/growth dynamics, plays the key role in defect formation. In situ grazing-incidence wide-angle X-ray scattering (GIWAXS) was used in real-time to probe $\mathsf{M A P b l}_{3}$ structural evolution during solution processing including one-step or two-step, and from lab-to-fab (e.g., spin-coating vs. blade-coating) to understand the relationship between solution-processing and film quality at a fundamental level.[11-15] For instance, intermediate phases in the precursor film are generated before the formation of perovskites such as MAI- $\mathsf{\cdot P b l}_{2}$ -DMF or MAI-PbI2-DMSO.[16-17] Perovskite crystals nucleate and grow from intermediates when solvents escape during film annealing because they are thermally unstable. In contrast to $\mathsf{M A P b l}_{3}$ perovskite, FAPbI3-based perovskite ( $\mathsf{F A}=$ formamidinium) crystallization dynamics are much more difficult to control because the chemical reaction from precursors to perovskite and the physical conversion between the $a-$ and δ-phases are involved. Attempts have been made to understand the conversion pathway of precursors – $\mathsf{F A P b l}_{3}$ during one-step spin-coating and the direct phase transition pathway of $\delta\mathrm{-}\mathsf{F A P b l_{3}}-\mathsf{a\mathrm{-}F A P b l_{3}}$ 3 during annealing using in situ GIWAXS.[18] The formation dynamics of $\delta\mathrm{-}\mathsf{F A P b l}_{3}$ can be regulated by introducing an alkali metal, thiocyanate $(\mathsf{S C N}^{-})$ , or bromide $(\mathsf{B r}^{-})$ ions.[19-21] Later, Jen et al found that MA-based additives can induce the formation of pure $\mathfrak{a}$ -phase $\mathsf{F A P b l}_{3}$ and dramatically reduce phase-transition temperatures.[22] Seok also found that the $\delta-$ to $\mathsf{a-F A P b l}_{3}$ phase
