transition rate can be changed by introducing alkylammonium chlorides, with better crystallinity and preferred crystal orientation.[23] These are line with our recent findings that $\delta-$ to – $\mathfrak{a}$ -FAPbI3 crystallization dynamics can be regulated by introducing additives that interact with precursors, e.g., to in situ passivate undercoordinated- $\cdot\mathsf{P b}^{2+}$ or to provide a skeleton for nucleation/growth of 3D perovskites during film formation.[24-25] Although great progress has been made, how polytypes nucleate and grow during phase conversion and how/when defects are formed and can be regulated during different conversion pathways have not been well explored. This is extremely important for developing efficient strategies to mitigate intrinsic defects to enable the fabrication of single-junction solar cells with an efficiency close to $30\%$ .
Here, we have systematically studied the crystallization dynamics of $\mathsf{F A P b l}_{3}$ perovskite during both solution-spinning and thermal annealing processes using extensive in situ characterizations and identified one key issue in the formation of stacking defects of $\mathsf{F A P b l}_{3}$ is how the intermediates participate in the crystallization process. This work revealed four stages for commonly observed conversion pathway of precursors – δ-FAPbI3 intermediate – $-a-F A P b|_{3}$ , during which spontaneous generation of stacking defects was directly demonstrated. This could provide helpful guidance for the rational design of crystallization pathways to in situ suppress the stacking defects for high-performance solar cells. Further, we obtained a novel conversion pathway of precursors – solvated intermediate – α-FAPbI3 by controlling the precursor and solvent interaction to suppress the $\delta\mathrm{-}\mathsf{F A P b l}_{3}$ intermediate. Next, we gained insight into how the changed conversion pathway modulates $(a-F A P b\vert_{3}$ nucleation/growth kinetics, thereby self-eliminating stacking defects during crystallization and improving morphology and trap concentration in the solid state. With the above understanding, the optimized films with in situ self-eliminated stacking defects achieved a PCE of $25.14\%$ at room temperature (300 K). Particularly such rational design of crystallization dynamics helps achieve better charge de-trapping at low temperatures. PCE as high as $26.12\%$ can be achieved at $240~\mathsf{K}$ which is close to circumventing near-space and polar regions. Therefore, these findings highlight the importance of phase conversion control for defect-free solar cells and the emergence of a new era for PSCs with near-space and polar regions.
## Results and discussion
## Structural evolution during film formation
The room-temperature ionic liquid (IL), 1-ethyl-3-methylimidazolium hydrogen sulfate $(E M I M H S O_{4})$ , was selected to control the polytypes and stacking defects of the $\mathsf{F A P b l}_{3}$ perovskite during crystallization. We first explained our design strategy for selecting EMIMHSO4 for phase-conversion control. Figure 1a shows the electrostatic surface potential map of $E M I M H S O_{4}$ (top). Electrons dominate at ${\mathsf{H S O}}_{4}^{-}$ anions while nuclei dominate at N atoms, indicating the electronic–donor and electronic– acceptor roles of EMIM+ and ${\mathsf{H S O}}_{4}^{-}$ , respectively. This interaction with $\mathsf{P b}^{2+}$ is supported by
