At the initial spinning state $\left(\operatorname{t}<30~\mathsf{s}\right)$ , broad and weak scattering rings at low $q$ values $(\sim0.2\ –0.5\mathring{\mathsf{A}}^{-1})$ were observed, which were associated with the colloidal state of the sol–gel precursor and were previously observed when solution-casting MAPbI3 from DMSO or DMF solvents.[14, 27-28] The colloidal state was thereafter transformed into a crystalline phase at $\mathtt{t}>\tilde{\mathbf{\xi}}\mathtt{N}30$ s when DMSO/DMF was extracted. The EMIMHSO4 starts to play a role in the formation of polytypes. The 6H (101) peak of the δ-FAPbI3 phase $\left(q=0.83\mathring{\mathsf{A}}^{-1}\right)$ ) dominates the control case, which is consistent with previous observations.[29] The EMIMHSO4 addition delays the formation of the 6H (101) phase from ca. 30 s to ca. 45 s, along with a strong appearance of additional diffraction features at $q=0.49\mathring{\mathsf{A}}^{-1}$ and $q=0.65\mathring{\mathsf{A}}^{-1}$ . These new peaks are ascribed to $\mathsf{F A P b l}_{3}$ -based solvated phases, e.g., $(\mathsf{F A})_{2}\mathsf{P b}_{3}\mathsf{l}_{8}-2\mathsf{D M S C}$ . Note that solvated phases are commonly observed for $\mathsf{M A P b l}_{3}$ -based systems during film formation owing to strong $\mathsf{P b l}_{2}$ –solvent Lewis acid adduct interactions.[14] However, solvates have rarely been found for the $\mathsf{F A P b l}_{3}$ -based case because of the much stronger interaction between FAI and $\mathsf{P b l}_{2}$ than that of $\mathsf{P b l}_{2}$ –solvent.[18, 29] These observations indicate that the interaction between EMIMHSO4 and $\mathsf{F A P b l}_{3}$ alters the conversion pathway. The conversion from the precursors to the 6H (101) phase was efficiently suppressed during film formation with delayed onset and decreased intensity. Instead, solvates, which are rarely observed, are preferred.
The diffraction intensity of the polytypes is integrated at the end of spinning (Figure S3) and is shown in Figure 1d in terms of their evolution during film formation for the control and target cases. We observed a linear growth in the diffraction intensity of the 6H (101) phase during film formation in the control case. The growth rate of the 6H (101) phase significantly decreased with the $E M I M H S O_{4}$ addition. The solvates also exhibited a linear increase of diffraction intensities with time for $\boldsymbol{q}=0.49$ $\mathring{\mathsf{A}}^{-1}$ and $q=0.65\mathring{\mathsf{A}}^{-1}.$ , with a faster growth rate for the latter. The nucleation and growth behaviors of the 6H (101) phase and solvates were further investigated using the Avrami equation,[12, 30] and are plotted in Figure 1e.
