rate constant, and n denotes the Avrami constant. The 6H (101) phase exhibits the same Avrami exponent $\mathfrak{n}$ (3.8) irrespective of the $E M I M H S O_{4}$ addition, indicating homogeneous nucleation and three-dimensional (3D) growth behaviors of the 6H (101) phase and the negligible effect of the introduced additive on its intrinsic nucleation behavior.[31-33] The solvates for the target film exhibit n values of 2.8 and 1.9 for the peaks at $q=0.49\mathring{\mathsf{A}}^{-1}$ and $q=0.65\mathring{\mathsf{A}}^{-1}.$ , respectively, possibly indicating 2D and 1D growths for the former and latter, respectively.
To elucidate the crystal orientation of polytypes during film formation, the structural evolution is further compared along the in-plane and out-of-plane directions for the control (Figure S4) and target cases (Figure 1f), respectively. The corresponding orientation coefficient, $\mathsf{C}_{\mathsf{o r t}},$ defined as the in-plane diffraction intensity divided by the out-of-plane diffraction intensity, was calculated and plotted in Figure S5 for polytypes.[34] The $\mathsf{C}_{\mathsf{o r t}}$ values remained unchanged before the nucleation and growth of polytypes, followed by a linear decrease with time during crystallization. This indicates that the outof-plane crystal orientation is highly preferred for polytypes, irrespective of the different phase transition pathways with the EMIMHSO4 addition.
