## Stacking defects during conversion to $\pmb{\alpha}$ -FAPbI3
In situ UV–Vis absorption and PL spectroscopies were further performed in real time to investigate how the polytypes convert to ${\mathsf{a}}{\mathsf{-F A P b}}{\mathsf{I}}_{3}$ and stacking defects are formed during conversion. Figure 2a and Figure 2b show 2D contour maps of the UV–Vis spectra (top) and PL spectra (bottom) for the control and target cases during spinning and thermal annealing, respectively. Figure 2c compares the dynamic evolution of the absorption intensity at $550\mathsf{n m}$ for both cases. Figure 2d shows the dynamic evolution of $a-F A P b\vert_{3}$ PL intensity. The PL peak position and full width at half maximum (FWHM) are extracted using Gaussian fitting for the selected PL spectra (Figure S6) and are plotted in Figure 2e against time.
During spinning, the sol-gel precursors exhibited negligible absorption features in the 400–900 nm range prior to anti-solvent dripping (at ca. 10 s) (Figure 2a, b, left). The anti-solvent dripping leads to the nucleation and growth of polytypes (shown in Figure 1), as evidenced by the appearance of UV– Vis features and red-shift with time.[35] However, we did not observe PL signals from these polytypes, except for the time slot of anti-solvent dripping. Polytypes are converted to $\mathsf{a-F A P b l}_{3}$ during thermal annealing (Figure 2a, b, right), during which three stacking processes are registered, as explained below.
