Fast crystallization at stage I. UV–Vis absorption and PL intensities of $a-F A P b|_{3}$ increased rapidly at stage I, indicating fast conversion of polytypes to $a-F A P b|_{3}$ (Figure 2a, b, right). The onset of conversion increase from ca. 17 to 30 s with the EMIMH ${\mathsf{S O}}_{4}$ addition as evidenced by UV–Vis spectra (Figure 2c), indicating slower dynamics for the solvates – $a-F A P b|_{3}$ conversion in contrast to the δ-FAPbI3 – α-FAPbI3 conversion. The delayed conversion is attributed to an additional solvent – FA exchange process during the solvates – $\mathfrak{a}$ -FAPbI3 conversion, which was not involved in the δ-FAPbI3 – α-FAPbI3 conversion. This delayed conversion dynamics is further confirmed by $a-F A P b l_{3}$ PL evolution (Figure 2d). It took ca. 28 and 65 s to reach the first emission peak at stage I for the control and target samples, respectively. The $a-F A P b|_{3}$ PL peak exhibits a red-shift from ca. 745 to $760\mathsf{n m}$ at stage I for both the control and target films (Figure 2e), along with a significant decrease in the FWHM values from ca. 110 to $65~\mathsf{n m}$ . This implies that $a-F A P b|_{3}$ crystals grow larger with less exciton quantum confinement in this stage.[36] These observations indicate that fast conversion from polytypes to $a-F A P b|_{3}$ occurs during the initial stage of thermal annealing.[37] The EMIMHSO4 addition changes the conventional conversion pathways from δ- $\mathsf{F A P b l_{3}}-\mathsf{a}\mathrm{-}\mathsf{F A P b l_{3}}$ to solvates – $\mathfrak{a}$ -FAPbI3, along with significantly delayed conversion dynamics.
Crystallization–dissolution equilibrium at stage II. We observed a continuous increase in the α- FAPbI3 absorption intensity (Figure 2c) while a significant drop in the $\mathfrak{a}$ -FAPbI3 PL intensity (Figure 2d). This indicates that when solvents escape from the bulk film, polytypes continue conversion to $a-F A P b|_{3}$ in the bulk film while the $a-F A P b|_{3}$ at the surface becomes partially dissolved. Moreover, we observed a slower increase trend of $a-F A P b l_{3}$ absorption intensity with the $E M I M H S O_{4}$ addition, which is similar to the delayed conversion at stage I and further indicates that energy is required to complete the solvent – FA exchange process. Despite the significant influence on the conversion pathways owing to the $E M I M H S O_{4}$ addition, a negligible effect on the dissolution was observed. For instance, it takes a close time-slot for $a-F A P b l_{3}$ at the surface to be dissolved for both the control and target films (ca. 40 s vs. 35 s). Meanwhile, the remaining $a-F A P b|_{3}$ crystals at the surface continue to grow, as confirmed by the slow red-shift of the PL peak (Figure 2e), with a slight variation $(<5\mathsf{n m})$ of the FWHM value. These observations indicate that the EMIMHSO4 addition still plays an important role in the conversion dynamics when solvents escape from the bulk film, while contributing negligibly to the crystallization– dissolution equilibrium of $\mathfrak{a}$ -FAPbI3 crystals at the surface.
Recrystallization and defect formation at Stage III. Further thermal annealing led to the recrystallization of the dissolved substances at the surface. For $a-F A P b\mid_{3}$ a maximum absorption intensity is almost reached without any clear variation with time (Figure 2c). However, different PL evolutions were observed during recrystallization. The PL intensity exhibits a rapid increase at ca. 78 s of thermal annealing, followed by a slight and continuous decrease at $>100\ s$ for the control sample, with a ca. 22 s time-slot for recrystallization. The decrease of $a-F A P b l_{3}P L$ intensity could be ascribed to the formation of stacking defects, which are caused by unfavorable volatile ion loss and lead to increased non-radiative recombination.[38-39] The EMIMHSO4 addition lead to a delayed onset of recrystallization at ca. $100\ s$ (Figure 2d). The delayed onset of recrystallization is consistent with the observations during fast crystallization at stage I and is also ascribed to the changed conversion pathways with the EMIMHSO4 addition. It is important to note that, the PL intensity exhibited a
