## Film characterizations
We performed film characterizations to investigate how the conversion pathway influences the final film state. Scanning electron microscopy (SEM), images exhibit an increased grain size of perovskite from 0.67 to $1.11~{\upmu\mathrm{m}}$ with the $E M I M H S O_{4}$ addition (Figure 4a), along with a decreased surface roughness from 30 to $22{\mathsf{n m}}$ as confirmed from atomic force microscopy (AFM) height images (Figure S7). We observe fewer diffraction features from $\mathsf{P b l}_{2}$ $(q=0.90\mathring{\mathsf{A}}^{-1})$ and 6H (101) phase $(q=0.83\ \mathring{\mathsf{A}}^{-1})$ with the $E M I M H S O_{4}$ addition from GIWAXS patterns (Figure 4b), indicating that the precursors – solvates – $\mathsf{\alpha}_{\mathsf{a}-\mathsf{F A P b}\mathsf{l}_{3}}$ conversion is beneficial for suppressing the undesired 6H (101) phase in the film. The UV–Vis absorption and PL spectra indicate that the conversion pathway has a negligible influence on the $a-F A P b|_{3}$ bandgap while significantly decreasing the defects and the consequent charge nonradiative recombination loss of the film (Figure 4c). Time-resolved PL (TRPL) spectra exhibit significantly prolonged carrier decay from ca. $0.52~\upmu s$ to ca. $0.93~\upmu s$ of the film post the $E M I M H S O_{4}$ addition (Figure 4d, Table S1). The significantly increased carrier lifetime of $a-F A P b|_{3}$ is beneficial for charge transport efficiency in a complete solar cell, with the expectation of increased open-circuit voltage $(V_{\mathrm{{oc}}})$ .
We further measure the trap density of the films using the space-charge limited current (SCLC) technique and present the dark $\jmath{-}V$ curves in Figure S8. The trap density $N_{\mathsf{t r a p}}$ was determined using the following equation: $N_{\mathrm{trap}}=(2\varepsilon\varepsilon_{O}V_{\sf T F L})/(e L^{2}).$ where $\varepsilon$ denotes the relative permittivity, $\scriptstyle{\varepsilon_{O}}$ denotes the vacuum dielectric constant, $V_{\mathsf{T F L}}$ denotes the trap-filled limit voltage, $e$ denotes the elemental charge, and $\iota$ denotes the thickness of the perovskite film.[41-42] The $N_{\mathsf{t r a p}}$ value decrease from $1.06\times$ $10^{16}$ to $5.02\times10^{15}~\mathrm{cm}^{-3}$ with the EMIMHSO4 addition (Figure 4e), which is consistent with in situ observed self-elimination of stacking defects during solution-processing. Mott–Schottky measurements are also performed to investigate the influence of the conversion pathway on the builtin potentials $(V_{\mathrm{bi}})$ of the perovskite films (Figure 4f). The $V_{\mathsf{b i}}$ value increased from 1.09 to $\phantom{-}1.18\:\mathsf{V}$ with the $E M I M H S O_{4}$ addition, from which a higher $V_{\mathrm{{OC}}}$ is expected in complete solar cells.[43, 44] Figure ${\pmb4}{\pmb g}$ shows the Nyquist plots and equivalent circuit model for the control and target devices. Compared to the control (Table S2), the target device demonstrates a smaller series resistance $\left(R_{s}\right)$ and a larger recombination resistance $\left(R_{\mathrm{rec}}\right)$ , which are indicative of a more enhanced charge transport and an effective barrier against charge recombination, thus favoring the device performance.[45-47] These results indicate that the change of conversion pathway from precursors – $-\Delta-F A P b l_{3}-a-F A P b l_{3}$ to precursors – solvates – $\cdot a-F A P b\vert_{3}$ is beneficial for decreasing trap defects and device resistance.
