and investigated at different operating temperatures for near-space and polar region applications. The current density–voltage $\left(J-V\right)$ was recorded under a standard AM 1.5 G solar simulator illumination with a scan rate of $20\mathsf{m}\mathsf{V}\mathsf{s}^{-1}$ . The influence of the EMIMHSO4 concentration on the device performance is investigated at room temperature (300 K), as shown in Figure S9. The highest PCE of $25.14\%$ is achieved for the target device (with 2 mol% EMIMHSO4, which is higher than that of the control device $\mathsf{P C E}_{\mathsf{m a x}}$ of $22.20\%$ ) (Figure 5b, Figure S10a, and Table S3). The PCE enhancement is mainly due to improvements of $V_{\mathrm{{OC}}}$ from $\pmb{1.13\vee}$ to $\ensuremath{1.18\mathrm{~V~}}$ and fill factor (FF) from $78.07\%$ to $83.05\%$ . Negligible hysteresis is observed for the target device under reverse and forward scans (Figure S11). Figure S12 shows the stabilized power outputs (SPO) of the control and target devices for 600 s with steady-state PCE of $25.05\%$ and $21.92\%$ , respectively.
The $\jmath{-}V$ curves and photovoltaic parameters of the control and target devices operating at low temperatures $(220-290~\mathsf{K})$ are shown in Figure S13, Table S5, and Table S6, respectively. We obtain a champion PCE of $26.12\%$ with a $J_{\mathsf{S C}}$ of $25.81\mathsf{m A c m}^{-2}$ , a $V_{\mathrm{{OC}}}$ of $\begin{array}{r}{1.22\mathsf{V},}\end{array}$ and a FF of $82.83\%$ for the target device at 240 K (Figure 5c), which significantly outperforms the control device (PCE of $23.55\%$ , Figure S10b). Moreover, we observed notable improvement of FF from $81.74\%$ to $82.83\%$ followed by a decrease to $71.90\%$ , a continuous $V_{\mathrm{{OC}}}$ from 1.18 to $\ensuremath{1.24\mathrm{~V~}}$ due to self-induced heating effects mechanism,[9, 48, 49] and negligible variation of $J_{\mathsf{S C}}$ with decreasing temperatures. The PCE statistical histograms of the 20 devices are shown in Figure 5d, demonstrating good reproducibility. The external quantum efficiency (EQE) curves of the control and target samples are shown in Figure S14. The integrated $J_{\mathsf{S C}}$ values are 25.19 and $25.77~\mathsf{m A}~\mathsf{c m}^{-2}$ for the control and target devices, respectively, which are extremely close to the values obtained from the $\jmath{-}V$ curves. To the best of our knowledge, this PCE value has reached the world’s top level for single-junction PSCs under standard AM $1.56$ solar simulator illumination, irrespective of the operating temperatures (Figure 5e).[5, 9, 50-52]
Light-dependent $V_{\mathsf{O C}}$ measurements were performed to investigate the charge carrier behavior within the devices. Fitting was performed using the following equation: $V_{\mathrm{OC}}=({\sf n k_{B}T/q})|{\sf n(1)}+\sf B,$ where n denotes the ideality factor, $k_{\mathsf{B}}$ denotes the Boltzmann constant, q denotes the elementary charge, and T denotes the absolute temperature.[53] The slope of the fitting line decrease from 1.22 to $1.12~\mathsf{k_{B}}\mathsf{T/q}$ for the control and target devices operating at $240\mathsf{K}$ (Figure 5f). A similar decreasing trend is observed at $300~\mathsf{K}$ with the EMIMHSO4 addition (Figure S15). The reduced ideality factor (n) demonstrates less defect-assisted nonradiative recombination for the target PSC modified by the EMIMH $150_{4}$ additive, which is consistent with the significant improvements in $V_{\mathsf{O C}}$ and FF.
