under a relative humidity of $20\text{\textperthousand}$ and 300 K. The target device retained $\sim90\%$ of its initial PCE after $1200{\mathsf{h}}_{\cdot}$ , outperforming the control device ( $90\%$ vs. $70\%$ ). Figure S17 shows the accelerated thermal stability test of unencapsulated devices at $358~\mathsf{K}$ in a nitrogen environment. The Poly[bis(4- phenyl)(2,4,6-trimenthylphenyl)amine] (PTAA) was used to replace Spiro-OMeTAD to remove other sources of instability. The target device maintained $80\%$ of its initial value after $450\mathsf{h}$ , which was higher than that of the control device ( $80\%$ vs. $60\%$ ). These observations indicate higher stability of $a-F A P b|_{3}$ converted from solvates instead of $8\mathsf{-F A P b}\mathsf{l}_{3}$ and a high potential for polar region or near-space applications.
