## WILEY-VCH
In summary, we present a full picture of the structural evolution of $\mathsf{F A P b l}_{3}$ perovskite during solutionprocessing using in situ diagnostics for $26.12\%$ -efficiency single-junction solar cells and demonstrate how polytypes and stacking defects are formed and regulated during different phase conversion pathways. We reveal that the dynamically favorable conversion pathway of precursors – δ-FAPbI3 intermediate– $a-F A P b|_{3}$ inevitably generates stacking defects. Such a conversion pathway can be changed to precursors – solvated intermediates – $\mathfrak{a}$ -FAPbI3 by controlling the precursor–solvent interactions. In addition, the change of the conversion pathway lead to significantly prolonged crystallization dynamics of $a-F A P b|_{3}$ because of the additional exchange process between the solvent and FA, and in situ self-elimination of stacking defects during prolonged $a-F A P b|_{3}$ crystallization. Therefore, the resultant optimized films achieved a PCE of $25.14\%$ and $26.12\%$ for solar cells operating at 300 K and $240\ \mathsf{K},$ respectively. These results bridge the solution-processing and resultant films/devices for high-performance PSCs and demonstrate how understanding of structural evolution can lead to rational regulation of stacking defects. However, this study did not explore the relative positions of stacking defects during the nucleation and growth of $a-F A P b1_{3}$ . Further studies are required to focus on the position and dynamic evolution of these stacking defects during crystallization. Overall, these findings provide a constructive way to in situ eliminate intrinsic stacking defects during the fabrication of high-performance solar cells and might open up a new era of application of this emerging photovoltaic technology to extreme environments such as near-space and polar regions.
